text:
I'm thrilled about "Oppenheimer" as it combines two subjects I'm passionate about: the history of the Manhattan Project and Christopher Nolan's filmography. To build anticipation for the premiere, I've compiled 30 spoiler free facts that I'll be sharing daily until the theatrical release. Half of these facts (even numbers) delve into Oppenheimer's life, offering glimpses into his character. The other half (odd numbers) shed light on Nolan's production process for the film.  In my history classes, I honed my summarization skills, condensing information to the essential elements with as few words as possible, and I can now apply that exercise here.

&amp;#x200B;

&amp;#x200B;

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*1/30\*\* - Oppenheimer marks Nolan's first movie without Warner Brother’s since his 2nd film Memento back in the year 2000. After Memento, Warner Brothers distributed all 9 of his next films. Nolan became unhappy with Warner Bros when right before the 2020 opening of blockbuster, Tenet, the studio suddenly announced that their movies would simultaneously release both in cinemas and on HBO Max. In light of this, Nolan chose Universal Pictures to produce Oppenheimer, as they were the only studio willing to meet all his requirements: a production budget of $100 million, an additional $100 million for marketing, complete creative control, 20 percent of the gross revenue, and an exclusive theatrical release window of 100 days. Warner Bros responded by scheduling, Barbie, a $100 million budget film of their own to premiere on the exact same day as Oppenheimer for a big budget showdown.

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*2/30\*\* - Oppenheimer's exceptional intelligence showed early on. From ages 7 to 11, Robert’s sole obsessions in life became mineral research and poetry. By the age of twelve, he began utilizing the family typewriter to correspond with renowned geologists regarding rock formations he had discovered in Central Park, New York. Unaware of his age, one of the recipients nominated Robert for membership in the esteemed New York Mineralogical Club. A letter even arrived inviting him to deliver a lecture before the club. Robert begged his father not to have him go, but his father found the entire scenario greatly amusing and accompanied Robert to the event. Upon his introduction to the podium, the startled audience erupted in a roar of laughter. Nevertheless, Robert read through his prepared remarks that was received with a standing ovation.

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*3/30\*\* - Though Oppenheimer is technically Nolan’s first biographical feature, he had planned to make a film about Howard Hughes in the early 2000s, with Jim Carey as the lead actor. “I think it’s the best script I’ve ever written”, reflected Nolan in 2014, “and I had a really wonderful experience writing it. The frustrating part came when the other movie got made first”. Nolan referred to Martin Scorsese making his own Howard Hughes film, The Aviator, before Nolan could begin production. Rather than compete with Scorsese on the same subject, Nolan decided to pivot to another project: Batman Begins. Similar themes from the Howard Hughes script trickled into the character of Bruce Wayne. Nolan’s unmade feature also prepared him to tackle Oppenheimer. Nolan stated, "I think having written the Howard Hughes script was very important to having the confidence to take this on, particularly structurally, and just knowing that if I could find the right approach, then I would be able to just dive into the guy's life”. Both Hughes and Oppenheimer exhibited striking similarities. They possessed eccentric personalities within their respective fields and grappled with personal struggles in later years, despite their remarkable achievements. This is best summarized in the Oppenheimer trailer, "You're a dilettante, you're a womanizer, unstable, theatrical, neurotic."

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*4/30\*\* - Oppenheimer grew up in a privileged Jewish household in New York City where his parents owned a textile business. They spoiled Robert and his brother Frank, granting their every wish and providing them with the best education money could buy. Robert often displaying his intelligence in a boastful manner, making him unpopular with his peers. He once famously told a classmate, “Ask me a question in Latin, and I shall answer you in Greek”. Due to his unapologetic arrogance combined with his rigid, awkward physique, he found it difficult to relate to others and endured severe bullying. One incident at a summer camp involved his annoyed fellow campers stripping him naked and locking him in an icehouse overnight. Reflecting on his childhood experiences, Robert would reflect much later in life, “my luxurious childhood did not prepare me for the fact that the world is full of cruel and bitter things”. Feeling isolated and unloved, he became increasingly withdrawn and turned to his studies, stating to his brother, "I need physics more than I need friends." Immersing himself into academics, he excelled in high school, graduating at the top of his class and gaining admission to the best universities in the country. Opting for Harvard, Oppenheimer took full advantage of the available courses, exploring additional subjects such as mathematics, European literature, Eastern religion, and philosophy. His independent study efforts were so remarkable that he was admitted to the graduate physics program as a first-year undergraduate. It was during a thermodynamics graduate course that Oppenheimer developed his fascination with theoretical physics. After only three years at Harvard, Robert graduated Summa Cum Laude with a Bachelor’s in chemistry. Yet, Physics was that subject that captivated him and he yearned to study it with the best. With the help of ambivalent references from Harvard professors, he got admitted to Ernest Rutherford’s laboratory at Cambridge.

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*5/30\*\* - Producer Charles Raven, who had collaborated with Nolan on the Batman trilogy, approached him with the idea of making a movie about an atomic bomb, based on the Pulitzer Prize-winning biography "American Prometheus." Enthralled by the idea, Nolan began writing the script for a movie as soon as he completed the book. He found the complexities of Oppenheimer's character, along with the profound weight of the implications to be one of the most challenging scripts he had ever tackled. Plus, the book's many meticulous details along with the possibility of multiple movies within the book itself added to the test. Despite these perimeters, Nolan adhered to the factual accounts presented in the 722-page biography while simultaneously integrating his definite creative style. To approach the project, Nolan decided to adopt a new perspective by writing the script from a first-person point of view, something he had never before attempted. By placing himself in Oppenheimer's shoes, Nolan aimed to capture the immensity of the moment stating, “The idea was to witness how Oppenheimer envisioned this revolutionary transformation of physics. Cinema has often struggled to portray intelligence or genius in a way that truly engages audiences. I wanted to go on this journey alongside Oppenheimer, experiencing the story rather than passing judgment on him. You are faced with these irreconcilable ethical dilemmas with him.”

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*6/30\*\* - Robert lived through a rough emotional state while at Cambridge, where he missed the structured routine of lectures he experienced at Harvard. He disliked the monotonous experimental physics work required for the grad program and preferred theoretical study, which he unfortunately didn’t have time for after long days in the lab. The lack of book theory contributed to his emotional decline. He would often be found motionless in a classroom staring at an empty blackboard, repeating the phrase "The point is..." for hours. Fellow grads recollected seeing him lying on the floor, groaning, and muttering incoherently about unsolved science problems leading some to believe he was severely depressed. There were more incidents that indicated Robert was losing control emotionally. Witnesses reported an incident on a train where he was unable to concentrate on his thermodynamics studies due to a nearby couple engaging in intimate behavior. When the man left the car, Robert suddenly attempted to kiss the woman, but was in an instant so overcome with remorse that he fell on his knees, tears streaming, begging for forgiveness. He then again abruptly changed temperament and hurled a suitcase at the woman that missed her head by inches. Yet another incident occurred when Robert traveled to visit a former high school classmate in Paris. Noticing that Oppenheimer was feeling depressed, his friend tried to cheer him by breaking the news that he was to marry his girlfriend. This unexpected news triggered Robert and he hurled himself onto Fergusson and tried to suffocate him. Although Fergusson easily fended off the attack (with Oppenheimer weighing 130 lbs at the time), the episode cemented Robert as a person with deep psychological troubles. This incident further highlighted Robert's deep psychological troubles. Reflecting on that specific stage of life years later, Robert admitted that his inability to process his inner turmoil led to him contemplating ending his life.

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*7/30\*\* - In line with previous projects, Nolan aimed to minimize the use of CGI, especially with the task of visually representing the titular scientists’ inner thoughts on an atomic level. When sharing the completed screenplay with his visual effects supervisor, Andrew Jackson, Nolan emphasized the need to delve into Oppenheimer’s mind. Nolan stressed, “we have to find a way into this guy’s head. We’ve gotta see the world the way he sees it. We’ve gotta see the atoms moving. We’ve gotta see the way he’s imagining waves of energy, the quantum world. And then we have to see how that translates into the Trinity test. We must feel the danger, feel the threat of all of this. Let’s do all these things, but without any computer graphics.”

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*8/30\*\* - Things reached a climax with Robert’s emotional state in an incident with Robert’s tutor. Like most Cambridge students, Oppenheimer had an official counselor, Patrick Blackett. He was popular, good at lab experiments, and functional at systematic science - everything Robert was not. Blackett would later win the Nobel peace prize in 1948 for his work on Cosmic Rays. Patrick hoped to instill in Robert positive habits for better lab results. What developed in Robert, however, was hatred. During a school break, Robert traveled to Corsica with some fellow classmates to take a break from the his lab duties. During the expedition, his peers sensed that Robert seemed more agitated than normal. One night during dinner, a waiter informed Robert about the next ferry that was returning to the mainland. When his friends asked why he was suddenly leaving so early, Robert said he couldn’t bear to tell them. But after drinking more wine, he revealed everything. He vetted about his tutor. In detail he revealed how he loathed the man. Before leaving on the vacation, Robert confessed to injected poisonous chemicals into an apple where he left it on Blackett’s desk. Robert’s companions swiftly notified the Cambridge administration. Blackett had not eaten the apple, but once Cambridge got word that the alleged “poison” used was lethal, they amounted it to attempted murder. Fortunately for Robert, his father happened to be visiting campus that very week and successfully intervened convincing the university to not press criminal charges. Instead, Oppenheimer was put on probation and required to see a psychiatrist in London. Robert was diagnosed with dementia praecox with symptoms that now most closely associate with schizophrenia.

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*9/30\*\* - This movie holds the record for being the longest runtime among Nolan films, with an official duration of just over 3 hours. It surpasses his second longest film, Interstellar, by a 13-minute margin. “The film is so emotional, and it feels like a thriller,” added Emily Blunt, who stars as biologist and Oppenheimer’s wife Katherine. “It’s almost like he’s Trojan-Horsed a biopic into a thriller. It’s a pulse-racing experience, the whole thing really. I was just completely arrested by the story, the portrait of this man, and, I guess, the trauma of a brain like that. When it was over, I could not believe it clocked at three hours”.

&amp;#x200B;

\*\*10/30\*\* - During his four months in London, Oppenheimer sought help from three psychoanalysts. However, he attributed his recovery from depression to two significant factors: the bicycle tour of Corsica that he experienced with his friends, and the reading of Marcel Proust's novel *In Search of Lost Time*. In 1926, Oppenheimer left Cambridge after being invited by the renowned physicist Max Born to study at the University of Göttingen, the world's leading center for theoretical physics in Germany. While at Göttingen, Oppenheimer had the opportunity to interact with colleagues who would later collaborate with him on future projects. These successful individuals included Niels Bohr, Paul Dirac, Enrico Fermi, Edward Teller, and Werner Heisenberg. Oppenheimer's enthusiastic participation in discussions sometimes became overwhelming, leading him to take over seminar sessions with long orations that annoyed many other students. As a result, the students signed a petition to request Robert quiet down or be kicked out of class. Born left the signed letter out on his desk where Oppenheimer could read it, and as a result, Robert’s in class contribution minimized without a word being said by Born. Oppenheimer earned his Philosophy degree at the age of 23 and his PhD just a year later. During his time at Göttingen, he published numerous papers related to physics, making significant contributions to the emerging field of quantum mechanics. One of his most cited works was a paper co-authored with Max Born, known as the Born-Oppenheimer approximation: a groundbreaking advancement in using quantum mechanics to understand molecular behavior and advance precise calculations. Oppenheimer thrived during his time at Göttingen, and by the end of his stay there, he had become one of the leading figures in theoretical physics worldwide.

